{
  "id": "Compound::DB01185 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Fluoxymesterone, a synthetic androgen, is implicated in the treatment of breast cancer through its role as a therapeutic androgen. The evidence suggests that Fluoxymesterone, through its inclusion in the class of Androgen Receptor Agonists and Androstanes, interacts with the Androgen Receptor, which is known to play a role in the regulation of cell proliferation and tumor growth. This interaction is further supported by the observation that Methyltestosterone, a closely related compound, is capable of palliating breast cancer, indicating a potential mechanism by which androgens can influence cancer progression. The shared lowest common ancestors, such as 17Beta-Hydroxy Steroid and Steroid Compound, highlight a broader biological pathway where androgens exert their effects through steroid hormone receptors. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of androgen signaling in breast cancer, suggesting that Fluoxymesterone may have a role in therapeutic intervention through this mechanism.",
